# Terminal UIs on the screen with ExRatatui

## Overview

[ExRatatui](https://hexdocs.pm/ex_ratatui) builds terminal UIs in Elixir
on top of the Rust [ratatui](https://ratatui.rs) library. An app
describes each frame as a list of widgets (paragraphs, tabs, gauges,
charts…) and receives events: keys, mouse, resizes. Usually it runs in
a terminal. Here
[raster_ex_ratatui](https://hexdocs.pm/raster_ex_ratatui) draws its
cells as pixels on the phone's screen through `/dev/fb0`: touches arrive
as mouse events on the cell under the finger, and the volume and power
buttons as keys.

`NervesLivebookFP3.TUI` puts an app on the screen and takes it off
again. One app owns the screen at a time.

## Free the screen

Scenic (notebook 11) needs the screen and the touchscreen to itself
too. If its viewport is still running, stop it there first. Starting a
TUI detaches the IEx console from the screen by itself.

## Show the Dashboard

The Dashboard has two tabs. *Showcase* turns a lit 3D object and shows a
photo as real bitmaps at the screen's resolution, next to a live BEAM
readout drawn with ordinary cells. *Input* shows every touch and button
press as the app receives it.

```elixir
NervesLivebookFP3.TUI.start()
```

## Touch and buttons

| Input | In the Dashboard |
|---|---|
| Tap a tab title | Switch to that tab |
| Volume up / down | Next / previous tab |
| Drag on the 3D object | Turn it by hand |
| Swipe across the photo | Next / previous photo |
| Power | Quit; a fresh Dashboard starts |

On the *Input* tab, touching the four corners shows whether the touches
land under the finger. The hint line at the bottom of the screen lists
what the current tab does.

## The same app in Livebook

[kino_ex_ratatui](https://hexdocs.pm/kino_ex_ratatui) runs an ExRatatui
app in the notebook, in a terminal emulator in the browser. This is the
same `NervesLivebookFP3.TUI.Dashboard` module, as a second app with its
own state, driven by the keyboard: `tab` switches tabs, `s` changes the
3D object, `p` the photo, `space` pauses. A browser terminal has no
pixels to spare, so the 3D object and the photo fall back to half-block
characters.

```elixir
Kino.ExRatatui.new(NervesLivebookFP3.TUI.Dashboard, height: "600px")
```

## Write an app

Any `ExRatatui.App` can take over the screen. This counter goes up with
a tap or volume up, down with volume down, and back to zero with the
power button (it arrives as `ctrl+q`).

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Counter do
  use ExRatatui.App

  alias ExRatatui.Event.{Key, Mouse}
  alias ExRatatui.Layout
  alias ExRatatui.Layout.Rect
  alias ExRatatui.Style
  alias ExRatatui.Text.{Line, Span}
  alias ExRatatui.Widgets.{BigText, Block, Paragraph}

  @impl true
  def mount(_opts), do: {:ok, %{count: 0}}

  @impl true
  def render(state, frame) do
    area = %Rect{x: 0, y: 0, width: frame.width, height: frame.height}

    [_top, number, hint, _bottom] =
      Layout.split(area, :vertical, [{:fill, 1}, {:length, 8}, {:length, 2}, {:fill, 1}])

    [
      {%Block{
         title: " Demo.Counter ",
         borders: [:all],
         border_type: :rounded,
         border_style: %Style{fg: :light_cyan}
       }, area},
      {%BigText{
         lines: [%Line{spans: [%Span{content: Integer.to_string(state.count)}]}],
         alignment: :center,
         style: %Style{fg: :light_yellow}
       }, number},
      {%Paragraph{
         text: "tap or vol up +1, vol down -1",
         alignment: :center,
         style: %Style{fg: :gray}
       }, hint}
    ]
  end

  @impl true
  def handle_event(%Mouse{kind: "down"}, state), do: {:noreply, %{state | count: state.count + 1}}

  def handle_event(%Key{code: "tab", kind: "press"}, state),
    do: {:noreply, %{state | count: state.count + 1}}

  def handle_event(%Key{code: "back_tab", kind: "press"}, state),
    do: {:noreply, %{state | count: state.count - 1}}

  def handle_event(%Key{code: "q", kind: "press", modifiers: ["ctrl"]}, state),
    do: {:noreply, %{state | count: 0}}

  def handle_event(_event, state), do: {:noreply, state}
end
```

Swap the Dashboard for it:

```elixir
NervesLivebookFP3.TUI.stop()
NervesLivebookFP3.TUI.start(app: Demo.Counter)
```

Change the colours or the hint in `render/2` and evaluate the module
cell again: the running app picks up the new code on its next render, at
the next tap. The same module also runs in the notebook:

```elixir
Kino.ExRatatui.new(Demo.Counter, height: "300px")
```

## Stop

Stopping the TUI hands the screen back to the IEx console, and frees
it for Scenic (notebook 11). `NervesLivebookFP3.TUI.start()` brings the
Dashboard back.

```elixir
NervesLivebookFP3.TUI.stop()
```
